WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.140
For every horror movie drawn from a screenwriter's
imagination, there's an equal number that

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.840
has been adapted from works of literary fiction
— and short stories in particular seem to

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.310
be particularly well-suited for a big-screen
debut.

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:17.250
Here are the most frightening horror movies
based on short stories.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:23.650
One of the most ferocious horror films of
the 1980s, Hellraiser was adapted by author-turned-director

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:28.090
Clive Barker from his own 1986 novella The
Hellbound Heart.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:33.010
Barker has said in interviews that the reason
he wrote and directed Hellraiser was to exert

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:37.390
some creative control over adaptations of
his work, having been displeased with the

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:43.010
feature film versions of Rawhead Rex, and
what became of his own script for Underworld.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:48.329
As a result, Hellraiser closely follows the
plotline of Hellbound Heart, with only a handful

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:53.069
of changes: For example, in the novella, Kirsty
is a friend of the ill-fated Rory, whose brother

00:00:53.069 --> 00:00:57.780
Frank accidentally summons the Cenobites,
while in the film, Kirsty is the daughter

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.570
of Rory, who's renamed Larry.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:05.890
The name of the diabolical puzzle box that
conjures up the Cenobites is also slightly

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:10.450
different: In the novella, it's the Lemarchand
Configuration, which Barker dubs the Lament

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:12.289
Configuration in his script.

00:01:12.289 --> 00:01:18.009
And speaking of the Cenobites, their S&amp;M-inspired
body modifications are, for the most part,

00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:22.780
almost identical to their description in Barker's
story — right down to the mass of pins in

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:27.109
the creature which, while nameless in the
book and film, would eventually become known

00:01:27.109 --> 00:01:28.109
as Pinhead.

00:01:29.109 --> 00:01:30.109
H.P.

00:01:30.109 --> 00:01:34.649
Lovecraft's hugely popular 1927 short story
"The Color Out of Space" has been adapted

00:01:34.649 --> 00:01:42.159
to film several times, most notably Die, Monster,
Die! with Boris Karloff and 1987's The Curse.

00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:47.939
The most recent and, by many accounts, best
screen translation is 2019's Color Out of

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:51.719
Space, directed by South African filmmaker
Richard Stanley.

00:01:51.719 --> 00:01:56.539
Stanley's version sticks closely to Lovecraft's
story, which is about a meteorite that crashes

00:01:56.539 --> 00:02:02.819
on a New England farm — and unleashes bursts
of unearthly color that cause horrific mutations

00:02:02.819 --> 00:02:06.049
to the farm's flora, fauna, and family.

00:02:06.049 --> 00:02:11.140
There are a few changes here and there; for
example, Stanley's script adds some dark humor

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:14.780
and a dash of modern angst about climate to
the story.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:20.170
Most importantly, though, Color Out of Space
nails one of Lovecraft's core concepts: that

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:25.520
unimaginable monstrosities lurk just beyond
our realm of understanding, always waiting

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.490
for the right opportunity to unleash their
horrors on the world.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:35.130
All three film versions of The Thing owe their
existence to John W. Campbell's novella Who

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:36.230
Goes There?

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:41.800
First published in 1938 in the magazine Astounding
Science Fiction, the novella depicts the battle

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:46.630
between a research team in Antarctica and
the shape-shifting occupant of a nearby alien

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:47.680
spacecraft.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:55.410
Of the three film adaptations, it's John Carpenter's
classic 1982 movie The Thing that most resembles

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:59.930
the plot of Campbell's novella, from which
it borrows character names, the iconic blood

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:03.650
test sequence, and the alien's transformative
abilities.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:08.560
Notably, however, the story of The Thing didn't
actually end with Who Goes There?

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.470
In 2018, a box of Campbell's manuscripts were
discovered, which revealed that the novella

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:18.370
was merely the shortened version of a longer
novel, titled Frozen Hell.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:22.670
Curiously, Frozen Hell appears to be an earlier
version of the tale that would become Who

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:23.970
Goes There?

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:28.120
Campbell had seemingly hoped to write the
story as a full novel, but when he failed

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:33.420
to sell it, he re-edited it into a shorter
form and sold it to Astounding Science Fiction.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:39.170
The new, updated story was finally published
in 2019, and a year later, Blumhouse Productions

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:43.300
announced that a film version of the expanded
story is in the works.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:47.930
“Why don’t we just wait here for a little
while… see what happens.”

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:58.450
Three stories by British author Daphne du
Maurier were adapted into film by Alfred Hitchcock

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:05.010
– the novels Rebecca and Jamaica Inn, and
the 1952 short story "The Birds," which hit

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.140
the silver screen in 1963.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:11.830
Du Maurier was inspired after seeing a farmer
attacked by birds while plowing a field and

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:16.850
retained that image for her story, which pits
a Cornish farmer against a growing army of

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:18.910
enraged birds.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:22.870
Screenwriter Evan Hunter expanded upon du
Maurier's story by moving the action to the

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:27.910
Bay Area, adding numerous characters, and
striking a slightly more humorous tone for

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:29.430
the movie's early scenes.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:34.180
What both projects share, however, is a lack
of concrete reason for the birds to actually

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:39.240
revolt: Du Maurier's story suggests that the
birds are set off by changing weather patterns,

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:43.100
while Hitchcock alluded in interviews that
the attack was nature's revenge on man.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:47.750
Neither is ever confirmed, however, which
only makes the phenomena in both film and

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:50.060
story all the more chilling.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:57.900
One of the greatest screams in '50s science
fiction horror comes in 1958's The Fly, in

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:06.639
which a brilliant scientist has his genes
crossed with a housefly's.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:10.830
Author James Clavell wrote the script for
Kurt Neumann's film, but the idea itself came

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:15.240
from the short story of the same name by French-British
writer George Langelaan.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:20.300
The story, which appeared in a 1957 issue
of Playboy magazine, was adapted to film with

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.100
very few changes.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:26.310
The primary differences included the fact
that Langelaan's story takes place in France,

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.570
while Neumann's film is set in Canada.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:32.680
Clavell also spared the protagonist's wife,
played in the film by Patricia Owens, from

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:36.400
the more macabre fate suffered by the written
version of the character.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.920
Langelaan's story was also credited as the
inspiration for two so-so sequels, Return

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:47.750
of the Fly in 1959 and Curse of the Fly in
1965, as well as for David Cronenberg's 1986

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:48.780
remake.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:52.770
As it turned out, however, the latter movie
was only very loosely based on Langelaan's

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:54.170
original story.

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:58.151
In fact, writer and co-director Cronenberg
only really retained the concept of the fly

00:05:58.151 --> 00:06:03.770
in the transporter device in order to tell
his terrifying — and instantly iconic — tale

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:04.830
of body-horror.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:12.080
An Oscar nominee for Best Foreign Language
Film in 1966, the 1965 Japanese supernatural

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:16.580
anthology film Kwaidan draws inspiration from
the work of Lafcadio Hearn.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:22.780
Hearn was a Greek-Irish writer who wrote extensively
about Japanese myth in the late 19th and early

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:27.900
20th centuries, and his stories introduced
Western readers to the vast array of ghosts

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:30.790
and creatures that populated Japanese folklore.

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:35.510
Four of those stories were adapted for Kwaidan,
which was directed with stunning visual flair

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:38.360
by arthouse favorite Masaki Kobayashi .

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:43.040
Being over half a century old, the scares
here are milder than what you might have come

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.050
to expect from J-horror movies.

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:49.960
However, both Hearn and Kwaidan found inspiration
in a creature that will be very familiar to

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:51.360
fans of the genre.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:55.730
Specifically, the vengeful onryō featured
in two of Hearn's stories are similar to the

00:06:55.730 --> 00:07:01.320
revenge-obsessed spirits depicted in The Ring,
the One Missed Call series, and countless

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.370
other Japanese ghost movies.

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:09.770
Mario Bava's 1963 anthology film Black Sabbath
attributes its three tales of supernatural

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:15.290
horror to stories by three different authors,
whose last names should be familiar to avid

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:21.000
readers of 19th-century world fiction — de
Maupassant, Chekhov, and Tolstoy.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:23.150
But things aren't quite what they seem, here.

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:27.180
The Tolstoy credited with the source material
for "The Wurdulak" isn't War and Peace author

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:32.800
Leo Tolstoy but his second cousin, Alexei
Tolstoy, who was better known for his plays.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:37.100
And it wasn't Anton Chekhov who penned the
inspiration for "Drop of Water," but Ivan

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:41.440
Chekov — who isn't actually a real person
at all, but something of a joke by Bava and

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:42.639
his co-writers.

00:07:42.639 --> 00:07:47.030
As for Guy de Maupassant's involvement in
"The Telephone," some historians suggest that

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:52.060
this is again a fabrication — though Bava
historian Tim Lucas has suggested that the

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:56.979
episode bears a passing resemblance to de
Maupassant's story "The Horla."

00:07:56.979 --> 00:08:02.090
Notably, that latter story was itself made
into a movie: Diary of a Madman, which starred

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:05.960
Vincent Price and was released the same year
as Black Sabbath.

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:11.840
No matter who penned each story, Black Sabbath
is one of Bava's most enjoyable and spine-chilling

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:17.410
works — thanks in no small part, of course,
to the legendary Boris Karloff, who both narrates

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:20.389
the film and features in "The Wurdulak."

00:08:21.389 --> 00:08:26.030
Richard Matheson's name comes up countless
times in the history of horror, fantasy, and

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:28.260
science fiction in the 20th century.

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:33.079
He's linked to some of those genres' most
influential and popular films and television

00:08:33.079 --> 00:08:39.800
series, from The Twilight Zone and I Am Legend
to Star Trek and Bid Time Return, the novel

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:42.110
that became Somewhere in Time.

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:46.550
Matheson is also responsible in part for helping
to launch Steven Spielberg's film career with

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:49.889
the 1971 TV movie Duel.

00:08:49.889 --> 00:08:55.129
Matheson adapted his own short story, published
in Playboy in 1971, for the ABC Movie of the

00:08:55.129 --> 00:08:56.129
Week.

00:08:56.129 --> 00:09:00.730
The script, about a motorist pursued by a
faceless and relentless trucker, caught the

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:06.190
attention of Spielberg, who earned huge ratings
and widespread critical praise for his nail-biting

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:09.149
mix of suspense and motorized action.

00:09:09.149 --> 00:09:14.019
In fact, Duel was so popular that it was released
to theaters in international markets, which,

00:09:14.019 --> 00:09:17.850
in turn, helped to pave the way for Spielberg's
motion picture career.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:22.839
He later paid tribute to the big break afforded
by Duel by inserting the truck's monster-like

00:09:22.839 --> 00:09:27.540
roar into the finale of Jaws, when the shark's
body sinks into the ocean.

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:35.399
Horror fans, critics, and filmmakers alike
have cited cult filmmaker Nicolas Roeg's 1973

00:09:35.399 --> 00:09:39.940
feature Don't Look Now as one of the best
and most influential supernatural thrillers

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:41.740
of the 1970s.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:46.390
For the film, Allan Scott and Chris Bryant
adapted the short story of the same name,

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:50.480
written in 1971 by Hitchcock favorite Daphne
du Maurier.

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:54.540
Don't Look Now stars Donald Sutherland and
Julie Christie as grieving parents who come

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:58.869
to believe that their late daughter may be
trying to warn them about an impending threat.

00:09:58.869 --> 00:10:03.869
"She's with you, my dear, and she's laughing!"

00:10:03.869 --> 00:10:09.339
The script closely follows the source material's
storyline, including its shocking final revelation

00:10:09.339 --> 00:10:10.370
and conclusion.

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:14.689
Meanwhile, Roeg's unique editing and color
schemes have influenced countless filmmakers

00:10:14.689 --> 00:10:15.920
over the years.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:20.889
Among the many filmmakers who have paid homage
to Don't Look Now is Joel Schumacher, whose

00:10:20.889 --> 00:10:26.009
1990 film Flatliners even features an overt
reference to the deadly figure in red who

00:10:26.009 --> 00:10:28.119
features in Roeg's film.

00:10:29.119 --> 00:10:33.970
Stuart Gordon's Re-Animator features some
of the most gruesome scenes in any horror

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:35.769
film of the 1980s.

00:10:35.769 --> 00:10:40.470
You might be forgiven for thinking that Re-Animator's
more outrageous moments were conceived by

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:44.310
Gordon himself, but the source material, H.P.

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:49.749
Lovecraft's "Herbert West – Reanimator,"
is actually even gorier than the movie.

00:10:49.749 --> 00:10:54.160
Gordon's script streamlined Lovecraft's story
too, which takes place over the course of

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:58.770
several years and even takes its anti-heroes
to the battlefields of World War I in search

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:00.420
of bodies to re-animate.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:06.129
And the sheer nastiness of "Herbert West"
is more than a match for Gordon's supercharged

00:11:06.129 --> 00:11:11.740
on-screen gore: West's re-animated subjects
carry out multiple killing sprees and, at

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:14.620
one point, even turn cannibal.

00:11:14.620 --> 00:11:19.540
Lovecraft's story also features an animated
body without a head, which leads a zombie

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:23.140
army against the West in the blood-soaked
final chapter.

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:28.041
If anything, it seems like Gordon actually
had to reel in the violence for the movie

00:11:28.041 --> 00:11:33.399
version of Re-Animator — which is pretty
remarkable considering what did end up on

00:11:33.399 --> 00:11:34.399
screen.

00:11:35.399 --> 00:11:40.279
MGM's Freaks has shocked and amazed viewers
since its debut in 1932.

00:11:40.279 --> 00:11:45.730
The film, directed by Tod Browning, features
actual sideshow performers in a macabre Gothic

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:52.809
story of love, betrayal, and revenge; one
that has earned as much praise as it has condemnation.

00:11:52.809 --> 00:11:57.079
Numerous writers, including uncredited future
Oscar winner Charles MacArthur, worked on

00:11:57.079 --> 00:12:01.920
the script, which was inspired by Tod Robbins'
1926 story "Spurs."

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:05.230
But Freaks isn't actually all that similar
to "Spurs."

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:09.920
Anyone who reads the actual story will find
that very little connects the two projects,

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:14.740
beyond the carnival setting and the ill-fated
love triangle between three of its performers.

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:20.069
Horror fans may also be disappointed to find
that the fate of the story's so-called "normal"

00:12:20.069 --> 00:12:24.489
characters is somewhat less disturbing than
what happens to trapeze artist Cleopatra and

00:12:24.489 --> 00:12:27.259
strongman Hercules in Browning's film.

00:12:27.259 --> 00:12:28.480
Haven't seen the movie either?

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:32.319
Well, let's just say… things don't turn
out well.

00:12:32.319 --> 00:12:34.160
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:37.879
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
movies are coming soon.

00:12:37.879 --> 00:12:41.399
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

